{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q018_tal_effector_repeat_lengths","answer":"d2kq5a_, d3v6pa_ and d4cj9a_ share family a.298.1.1 because SCOPe classifies the TAL effector DNA-binding repeat domain, not a fixed-length chain. All three are built from the same unit: a tandem ~33-35 residue repeat that folds as a helix-turn-helix hairpin. The repeats stack into a right-handed superhelix (class a, all-alpha; fold and superfamily a.298 and a.298.1). Each repeat has a hypervariable repeat-variable diresidue (RVD) at about positions 12-13. These loops contact DNA bases. The rest of the repeat is highly conserved. The three entries are therefore connected by the same repeat architecture and the same conserved repeat sequence pattern. That is strong homology evidence at the family level.

The length differences come mainly from how many repeats each PDB construct contains. The entries also differ in whether they include flanking or cryptic repeat regions, and in whether the SCOPe domain was cut from a larger protein or a DNA complex. A domain with only a few repeats is about 50-100 residues. One with a full array of roughly 15-18 repeats can be 500 or more residues. Each added repeat adds about 34 residues. Repeat proteins are classified by the shared repeat unit and superhelical packing, not by total length or by a fixed number of repeats. Length is therefore not a criterion for separating families. I am not asserting exact repeat counts or boundaries for these three entries from memory. Check them against the SCOPe domain records.

Limits on interpretation: sharing a family says the repeat fold and mechanism are homologous. It does not mean the proteins have the same DNA target. Specificity depends on the RVD sequence and the repeat order, and it is not transferred by family membership. Repeat count, array length, and DNA-bound versus free superhelical pitch also vary. Using one entry as a template for another needs an explicit repeat-by-repeat alignment. Choosing a family representative should take into account how complete the array is, whether DNA is bound, and the resolution. Pairwise sequence identity or length between a short and a long domain is not a good guide to relatedness here.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}

